course: everyday
lesson: 90
title: Facing fears
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Fear is a normal feeling that warns us about danger, but sometimes it is much bigger than the real danger.
explain: Fears shrink when you know the facts, when you talk about them with someone you trust, when you have a plan, and when you face them in small steps.
explain: Slow breathing helps with every fear: breathe in through your nose and out slowly through your mouth, and your body calms down.
explain: A girl in Toronto is scared of the deep end; she learns with a swimming teacher, a little deeper each week, and by the summer she jumps in with a smile while the lifeguard watches.
fact: Walk past a barking dog slowly and calmly without staring or running, because running can make a dog chase you.
fact: Almost everyone feels stage fright; practising and slow breathing help, and the fear often fades once you start.
fact: Talking about a scary film with a parent makes the fear smaller, and films are made up with special effects.
fact: A vaccination stings for only a second, and the vaccine protects you from serious illnesses; telling the nurse, looking away and breathing out slowly helps.
fact: Asking the dentist to explain what they will do and agreeing a hand signal to pause makes a visit much less scary.
fact: In a thunderstorm, stay indoors away from windows; counting the seconds between the flash and the bang shows how far away the storm is: the longer the gap, the further away it is, so a growing gap means it is moving away.
fact: Most spiders and insects are harmless; keep your distance and ask an adult to move them, and in Australia some spiders need an adult.
fact: Courage in the water grows by small steps with a teacher, and you only swim where an adult is watching.
fact: Worries kept inside grow, so tell your parents about them and ask for the real facts.
fact: The news shows the worst things, so ask an adult to explain and limit how much news you watch.
fact: Agreeing a meeting place with your family before going into a crowd means you know what to do if you get lost; if you are lost, ask a staff member or a parent with children for help.
exercise: A big dog barks at you behind a fence. What do you do? | Stay calm and walk past slowly without running | Run past it as fast as you can so it cannot catch you | Stare it in the eyes to scare it | Bark back at it loudly
exercise: You are scared to go on stage in the school play. What helps? | Practise a lot and breathe slowly before you go on | Do not practise at all, so you have nothing to be nervous about | Pretend to be ill on the day | Think about everything that could go wrong
exercise: You cannot sleep after a scary film. What should you do? | Talk to a parent and do something calm | Watch another scary film | Hide under the bed all night | Keep it to yourself so nobody laughs at you for being scared
exercise: You are scared of the needle at your vaccination. What helps? | Tell the nurse, look away and breathe out slowly | Run out of the room before the nurse can even get started | Watch the needle closely the whole time | Hold your breath and tense your arm
exercise: Why is a vaccination worth a quick sting? | It protects you from serious illnesses | It makes your muscles stronger | It stops you getting any cold ever | It is only a test of bravery
exercise: You are afraid of the dentist. What can make it less scary? | Ask them to explain and agree a hand signal to pause | Keep your mouth shut tight the whole time so they cannot start | Never go to the dentist again | Eat lots of sweets beforehand
exercise: There is a thunderstorm and you are scared. What do you do? | Stay inside away from windows and keep busy | Go outside to watch the lightning | Shelter under a tall tree | Stand right by the window so you can count all of the flashes
exercise: Why does counting between the flash and the bang help? | It shows whether the storm is moving away | It makes the next thunder quieter | It stops lightning from striking | It makes the storm finish sooner
exercise: There is a spider in your bedroom in England. What is sensible? | Keep your distance and ask an adult to move it outside | Pick it up quickly and squash it in your hand before it bites you | Scream and throw shoes at it | Leave the house until it goes
exercise: You are scared to swim in the deep end. What is the safe way to get braver? | Learn step by step with a teacher and an adult watching | Jump in alone when no one is looking, so nobody sees you are scared | Never go near a pool again | Ask a friend to push you in
exercise: You keep worrying that something bad will happen to your parents. What should you do? | Tell them about the worry | Keep it inside so they are not upset | Check on them every few minutes all night | Pretend you are not worried
exercise: The news about a big fire is frightening you. What helps? | Ask an adult to explain and watch less news | Watch every single report all day so that you know everything | Stay awake listening for fire alarms | Stop going outside for good
exercise: You are afraid of getting lost at a busy fair. What should your family do? | Agree a meeting place before you go in | Hold hands for the whole day without letting go | Stay at home and never go to fairs again | Each go a different way to save some time
test: A boy in Leeds walks past a growling dog at a gate. Why should he not run? | Running can make a dog chase him | Running makes the dog bark louder | Dogs only chase children dressed in red | He would get too tired to run home
test: Why does stage fright not mean you will do badly? | Almost everyone feels it, and it often fades once you start | It means you have not practised enough and will forget your lines | It makes your voice louder and better | It only happens to bad actors
test: A girl in Arizona is lost at a big theme park. Who is a good person to ask for help? | A staff member or a parent with children | The first teenager she sees | Nobody; she should keep wandering around until she finds her family | A person in a car outside the gate
test: Why do frightening films not show real danger? | They are made up, with special effects | They are always about the past | Monsters only live far away | Films are filmed in the dark
test: A boy is scared of the dentist's drill. What would help him most? | Knowing what comes next and having a stop signal | Not going at all and just hoping that his teeth stay fine on their own | Biting the dentist's finger | Closing his eyes and keeping silent
test: What usually makes a worry smaller? | Talking about it with someone you trust | Keeping it a secret until it goes away | Thinking about it alone every night | Looking for more scary facts
test: During a storm, a boy counts five seconds between the flash and the bang, then ten seconds a bit later. What does it show? | The storm is moving away | The storm is coming closer | The storm has stopped for good | The lightning has hit his house
test: A girl in Sydney sees a large spider in the shed. What is the safe choice? | Keep away and tell an adult | Pick it up to show her friends | Poke it with her finger to see | Ignore it and keep working there
test: Why does facing a fear in small steps work? | Courage grows a little each time you succeed | Fear disappears the moment you start | Small steps make the danger bigger | It is quicker than doing it all at once, so the fear has no time
test: dog barking at me thru the fence, gonna leg it past | Walk past slowly and calmly without staring | Run as fast as you can past it | Stare right at it to show who is boss and make it back off | Bark back at it to scare it off
test: flash then i counted 1 2 3 4 5 6 then boom. whys counting help | It shows how far away the storm is | It makes the storm go away much faster | It tells you when the rain will stop | It shows how loud the next one will be
test: jab today 😬 gonna hurt so bad isnt it | It stings for a second; look away and breathe out slowly | It hurts all day long, so it is fine to cry as loudly as you like | It hurts for a week afterwards | Hold your breath and squeeze your arm
test: the big footy game tmrw, theres gonna be thousands of ppl, what if i get lost | Agree a meeting place with your family first | Just wander around until you find them | Stay home so you never get lost | Follow any family that walks past
explain: A girl in Florida was too scared to tell anyone she could not swim and went into the deep end with friends; a lifeguard had to pull her out.
explain: A boy in Leeds was afraid of the dentist and hid his toothache for weeks; by the time he went, the tooth needed taking out instead of a small filling.
fact: Hiding a fear sometimes leads people into danger, while telling an adult gets help.
fact: Fears usually get smaller when you face them in small, safe steps with support.
test: can't swim but too embarrassed to say, everyone's going in the deep end | Tell an adult or the lifeguard and stay in the shallow end | Go in anyway and copy the others, you'll pick it up | Jump in, you'll learn fast | Hold your breath and hope nobody notices you can't swim
test: scared of the doctor so i'm hiding that my arm hurts loads | Tell a parent anyway, hiding it can make it worse | Hide it until it stops, doctors only make things hurt more | Use the other arm forever | Only tell if it falls off
test: scared of dogs, so i run whenever i see one | Stand still and calm, running can make a dog chase you | Run even faster each time so the dog can never catch up with you | Scream so it goes away | Throw things at the dog
test: Ruby says her cousin got chased by a dog a mile down the road n it was like a wolf, foaming at the mouth | Walk past a barking dog slowly without staring or running; the wolf and foam bits are likely blown up | Always run as fast as you can from any barking dog, even if it is behind a gate | It must be true, all barking dogs are rabid | Stare the dog straight in the eyes to show who is boss, then it will run off scared
test: everyone's saying the flu jab at school has a needle THIS long n hurts for a week | A vaccination stings for only a second and protects you; the giant needle story is rumour | It is true, so it is best to hide on jab day | It is true, but the pain is worth it, because it only lasts a week and then it is gone | Vaccines do not protect from anything anyway
test: heard Noah wet himself in the haunted house at the fair, can i tell the class | No, do not spread it; being scared is normal and it would embarrass him | Yes, it is a funny story everyone should hear, and he will laugh about it too | Yes, but only to the people who were not there | Yes, if you change his name a bit
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of confronting a fear? | Fears generally diminish when they are faced in small and safe steps with support | Fears are invariably increased by any attempt whatsoever to confront them | Fears disappear entirely when they are concealed from parents and teachers | Fears may only be overcome by confronting them all at once, alone and entirely without help
exam: During a thunderstorm, identify the conduct consistent with the established facts concerning safety. | Remaining indoors at a distance from windows | Standing beneath a tall tree in an open field | Swimming in an outdoor pool until it has passed | Holding a long metal pole aloft in an open place
exam: Pursuant to the attendance of a large crowd by a family, which precaution is recommended? | A meeting place is agreed in advance, so that each member knows what to do if separated | Each member is instructed to remain wherever they happen to be lost until the evening has ended | No arrangement is made, as children are never separated from adults in a crowd | The youngest member is placed in charge of finding the others if anyone is lost
exam: In respect of frightening reports upon the news, select the response consistent with the established facts. | The news shows the worst events, so an adult is asked to explain and news viewing is limited | The news shows only typical events, so everything reported will soon happen near one's home | The news is entirely invented, and therefore no report of any kind need ever be considered | The news ought to be watched for as many hours as possible in order to reduce any fear
